# Naming: Supabase and RLS

Same rules as `naming.mdc`, which holds the core vocabulary; these names are public API (invariant 14).

- better-supabase: `permdock/better-supabase` fills better-supabase's runtime slots and names them after the slot, not the brand: `authorizationProvider` (its `authorization` config), `authorizationHooks` and `AuthorizationHooksOptions` (its `AuthorizationHooks`, the `authorization` option of `createServer`), `authorizer` (its `authorizer` server option, an `Authorizer` over `Permission` references that reads `subject.properties.claims`; `store` is the `ApprovalStore` that resumes `context.approval`), `bucketPolicy` and `topicPolicy` (its storage and realtime access policies, taking `Permission` references and either `{ manifest, catalog }` or `{ policy, schema? }`), `apiKeyVerifier` (a `CredentialVerifier` over its API keys block), `apiKeyClaimOptions` (its `api_key` claim settings from `rls.apiKeys`), `toolPolicy` (its `createMcp` `authorize` and `visible` hooks). `authorizer` and `toolPolicy` take `onError(error, { permission, tenant })`, a `BetterSupabaseOnError` that sees a thrown check and never changes the denial, `credentialGuard` (a `CredentialProvider` that PermDock guards), `accessReviewApplier` (applies revoked access-review items through its organizations and invitations blocks), `blockMembershipSource` (a `MembershipSource` over its memberships block table, with `roleThrough` or a `roles` junction; `tables` takes its `blockTables(config)` as returned), `subjectFromBetterSupabase` and `subjectFromShareLink` (a link subject from its share-links block's `resolve`, keyed by the row's `subject.type` in `links`). `authorizationProvider`'s `approvers` option is keyed by better-supabase's approval kind and fills its `isApprover` function; `approver` becomes the `'ai-tool'` entry, and the provider never sets `canApprove`. Its `personas: 'roles'` is sent as written with one `fixtureRoles` entry per role the manifest's `fixtureRoles` names. `activeTenant` (`BetterSupabaseActiveTenant`) takes better-supabase's `blocks.permissions.activeTenant` value as written. Its `definePermDock(policy, options)` returns a `BetterSupabasePermDock` whose `next()` is a `BetterSupabaseNext`: `authorization`, `authorizer` and `versions` for `createNext`, plus `getSnapshot(source, { tenant, omitStatic? })` (`BetterSupabaseSnapshotQuery`), which builds one tenant's snapshot; never a `getSnapshot` that ships every tenant. `apiKeyVerifier` shares its name with the one in `permdock/server` on purpose: both fill the same `CredentialVerifier` slot over a different key store. `permdock/supabase` stays plain Supabase and never names better-supabase ([better-supabase](../../apps/docs/content/docs/adapters/better-supabase.mdx)).
- Field security: RLS is row-level unless `rls generate --fields views` (`rls.fields: 'views'`) emits `<table>_visible`, a `security_invoker` view whose restricted columns are `case when <permitted> then col end` over the row policy's helper calls; `--revoke-columns` (`rls.revokeColumns`) keeps only unrestricted columns and the key readable on the table and reads the rest through the owner-rights `<table>_visible_fields` companion (joined on `permdock_key`, marked `permdock:field-companion`); `--fields mask` (`rls.fields: 'mask'`) adds `<schema>.<table>_mask(p_row)`, a `security invoker` function that nulls the restricted columns of one row by the same masks, for owner-rights SQL. Never Postgres column privileges per app role and never a permission per field. `rls import` returns `fieldViews`, `testRlsParity({ fieldViews })` compares with `pick`, doctor PD030 ([field security](../../apps/docs/content/docs/adapters/rls.mdx)).
- Memo and caches: `rls.memo` is `'none'` (default, no memo), `'permdock'` or `'better-supabase'`; `memoryVersionCache({ shared })` takes a `VersionStore` (`get`, `set`, `delete`, runner `testVersionStore`); `betterSupabaseVersionCache(cache)` wraps better-supabase's `createVersionCache`; the signed-in user's subject is `<helpers schema>.permdock_subject()`, and for one tenant `permdock_subject(p_tenant)` (better-supabase's `subjectForTenant`, `null` for a tenant the caller is no member of); `<helpers schema>.permdock_bootstrap_permissions(p_tenant)` returns the caller's keys as better-supabase's compact bootstrap wire. All three are executable by `authenticated` only.
- PowerSync member level: `powersync.memberLevel.<resource>` is `{ table, column }`, the membership column whose value `own`, `team`, `all` or null narrows a device's stream to the resource's `own` or `team` level (`PowerSyncMemberLevel`). It is a level, never a `scope`: scope is the tenancy name.
- Supabase definition: `definePermDock(policy, options)` from `permdock/supabase` adds `client`, `fromClaims`, `fromClient` and `getPermissions` to the app definition and never imports `better-supabase`. `subjectFromSupabaseClient` reads `getClaims()`, never `getSession()`. The `supabase()` generate plugin conflicts with `rls()` and `supabaseHook()`.
- Custom roles: `scope: 'global'` marks a platform custom role (`CustomRoleSource.globalRoles`), capped by assignable global roles; `shared: true` marks a shared custom role (`CustomRoleSource.sharedRoles`, `SharedCustomRole`), held in every tenant, whose name wins over a tenant role's (`name-taken`). `CustomRole.grants` entries are `{ permission, effect?, level? }` and carry no condition, approval or limit; `level` names a condition the resource declares in code (`resource(…, { levels })`), and an undeclared one is `unknown-level` and denies; `orderedLevels: true` on the resource lets a held level hand out every level declared before it. The version of a tenant's custom roles is `custom_role_version` in SQL, `custom_roles_version_for(p_tenant)` to read it (wrapper `<prefix>custom_roles_version_for`), the claim `custom_roles_ver`, `Principal.customRolesVersion`, `MembershipSource.customRolesVersion` and `SubjectRecord.customRolesVersions`; the compiled keys are `custom_role_compiled` and `permdock_recompile_custom_roles()`; one `authz-tenant:<tenant>` message goes out per edited tenant. `resolveCustomRole` is the only resolver (evaluation, `snapshot`, `snapshotFor`, `validateCustomRole`, doctor PD023); the ceiling is the code allows of declared `assignable` roles in the role's scope, never hosted grants. `assignablePermissions` / `useAssignablePermissions` sit next to `assignableRoles`; `meta.manageRoles` on a role or permission lifts the held-grant intersection ([custom roles](../../apps/docs/content/docs/concepts/custom-roles.mdx)).
- Static grants: `rls.staticGrants` is `'inline'` (default) or `'ref'`; `'ref'` pins the hash of a `policy-bundle` v1 document (`permdock policy bundle`, kinds `bundle` and `sql`, table `<schema>.permdock_policy_bundle`). A missing or mismatched bundle denies; never `rls.seeds: 'bundle'` or a flag on `rls generate`.
- RLS custom-role and seed names: `rls.customRoles.from.sync: 'trigger'` (`permdock_sync_custom_role_*`, with the before-row `permdock_sync_custom_role_guard_*` caller checks), `permdock_custom_role_guard(p_tenant, p_scope, p_scope_id, p_role)` and `permdock_custom_role_beyond(p_tenant, p_scope, p_scope_id, p_allow, p_deny, p_include)` as the stable checks for an app's own write path, `permdock_custom_role_name_guard` on `rls.customRoleWrites.roles`, `rls.customRoleWrites.roles.primaryKey` with `permdock_replace_custom_role_grants_by_id`, `permdock_trusted_replace_custom_role_grants_by_id` and `permdock_trusted_role_permissions_by_id`, `rls.customRoleWrites.audit` with the `permdock.custom_role.replaced` event, `permdock_custom_role_entry_problem(p_scope, p_entry)` with `parseCustomRoleEntry(policy, entry, { scope })` from `permdock`, the `RoleMap` role column `{ column, map }`, `rls.seeds: 'function'` with `<schema>.seed()`, `rls.seeds: 'view'`, `rls.tables.<resource>.via` with `permitted_<parent>_ids`, `permdock rls explain`, `supabase.hook.teams` with the `team_ids` claim, and doctor `PD072` and `PD073`. A seeded grant key is `<permission>`, `<permission>#<n>` or `<permission>#<group>` for a condition, or `<permission>@<level>` for a level, never both, and a custom role entry `<permission>[@<level>]` ([RLS](../../apps/docs/content/docs/adapters/rls.mdx)).
